    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `AcceptanceTest` passing).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe rule: when **more than 80** owners have already been created for the new owner's registration day, the created owner carries `bulkSignupWarning: true`; otherwise `false`. This value is returned on the create response and read back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nI followed the existing `namesakeCount` pattern (a value computed at create time against the pre-save owner population and persisted on the owner), since the warning depends on a global same-day count that a single owner can't derive on its own:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` response schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` and MapStruct mapping pick it up automatically).\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column field with getter/setter.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 set `bulkSignupWarning = countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate) > 80` before saving. I extracted a shared `countOwnersRegisteredOn(date)` helper and refactored `requireDailyCapacity` to use it, so the warning and the existing daily-limit rule (\u2265100) count the same population \u2014 matching the test's note that \"the >80 branch shares the same accumulation path exercised by the daily-limit rule.\"\n- **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column (tests run on hsqldb; the others kept consistent).\n\nThe threshold (80) sits below the daily cap (100), so the warning can surface for the 81st\u2013100th same-day owner while the create still succeeds. The acceptance test's fresh-create false branch passes, and the value persists for read-back on GET.",
