    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **Follows the established `namesakeCount` pattern.** `bulkSignupWarning` is modeled exactly like the existing `namesakeCount` field: a `@Column` on `Owner` with a getter/setter, computed and set once at create time in the controller, added to all four DB schemas, and exposed as a `readOnly` property in `openapi.yml`. A maintainer would find this the natural way to add such a field here.\n- **Good deduplication.** Extracting `countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate)` removes the inline count that previously lived only in `requireDailyCapacity`, and makes it the single shared definition for both the daily-limit rule and the new warning. That is the correct unit for this logic, and the Javadoc explaining the \"pre-existing owners, evaluated before save\" semantics is apt.\n- **Threshold is a named constant** (`BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD`) alongside the existing `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY`, consistent with how the controller already expresses its limits.\n- **No new catch-all.** The computation reuses the extracted helper and adds a single line in the create path; it doesn't bloat any class beyond the existing (pre-existing) shape of the controller.\n\nThe warning logic sits in the same layer as the already-present daily-capacity and per-city business rules, so its placement is consistent with the codebase's actual structure rather than introducing a divergent one.",
